# git-lfs
#
# 420 of the tracked files are binary artwork, and they account for ~263 MB of
# the ~286 MB of history -- for ~15 MB of code. Without LFS, every re-export of
# an overlay appends another full copy, forever.
#
# LFS is NOT enabled here, deliberately: turning it on converts these files on
# the next commit that touches them, and anyone cloning without git-lfs
# installed then gets pointer text files instead of images -- which means blank
# overlays on stream. It also does nothing about the ~263 MB already in history,
# which needs a separate, coordinated history rewrite.
#
# To adopt it, on a day with no event scheduled:
#   1. Confirm git-lfs is installed everywhere the repo is cloned, including the
#      broadcast machine.
#   2. `git lfs install`
#   3. Uncomment the lines below and commit them.
#   4. Optionally rewrite history with `git lfs migrate import --include="*.png"`
#      -- this changes every commit hash and requires a force-push and a fresh
#      clone everywhere.
#
# *.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.jpg  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.zip  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.mp4  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
